<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Learning from demonstration allows robots to acquire complex skills from human demonstrations, but conventional approaches often require large datasets and fail to generalize across coordinate transformations.
In this paper, we propose Prompt2Auto, a geometry-invariant one-shot Gaussian process (GeoGP) learning framework that enables robots to perform human-guided automated control from a single motion prompt.
A dataset-construction strategy based on coordinate transformations is introduced that enforces invariance to translation, rotation, and scaling, while supporting multi-step predictions.
Moreover, GeoGP is robust to variations in the user’s motion prompt and supports multi-skill autonomy.
We validate the proposed approach through numerical simulations with the designed user graphical interface and two real-world robotic experiments, which demonstrate that the proposed method is effective, generalizes across tasks, and significantly reduces the demonstration burden.
The project page is available at <a href="https://prompt2auto.github.io" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://prompt2auto.github.io</a></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learning from demonstration (LfD) has become a widely adopted paradigm in robotics, offering an intuitive way for humans to transfer skills to the robots without the need for explicit programming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
By observing and generalizing from human motion demonstrations, robots can acquire complex manipulation or navigation behaviors that are difficult to encode through conventional control and planning frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
This approach is particularly compelling in unstructured or dynamic environments, where manually designed policies often fail to handle the variability of the real-world conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent progress in LfD has been dominated by deep learning methods, which excel at extracting high-dimensional representations and modeling nonlinear mappings from human demonstrations to robot policies.
These approaches have achieved impressive results across different domains such as manipulation, grasping, and trajectory learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
However, their data-hungry nature poses a critical limitation, which is that collecting large-scale demonstration datasets is often costly, labor-intensive, and in many cases infeasible.
Moreover, retraining deep models to adapt to new tasks or environments introduces latency that undermines their practicality for online or adaptive robot learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.14040v1/fig/first_pic.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/489;" width="476" height="489" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the process from motion prompt to automated control: (a) the expert guides the robot along the desired reference trajectory (green), (b) the operator provides a partial motion prompt (blue), and (c) the GeoGP predicts the remaining trajectory, enabling automated robot control.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, Gaussian Process (GP) regression has emerged as a data-efficient solution for LfD.
As a non-parametric method, it achieves strong generalization with significantly fewer samples than deep learning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
In addition, GPs learn effectively from limited data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, support online adaptation through incremental updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and provide calibrated uncertainty estimates that enable safe decision-making in unstructured environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
These strengths have led to many applications in human motion prediction<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, pose learning with dual quaternions<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and trajectory prediction for domains such as ship navigation<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and space debris tracking<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Despite these successes, existing GP-based approaches for LfD remain limited in their ability to generalize to unseen but geometrically similar motions.
In particular, they often fail to reproduce a trajectory when its starting point is shifted or when the query trajectory exhibits variations without data argument techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, which restricts their applicability in practical scenarios.
Furthermore, most existing GP approaches assume access to multiple demonstrations, whereas in many real-world settings, only a single demonstration is available, especially when data collection is costly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce a novel framework for geometry-invariant one-shot GP (GeoGP) learning that enables robots to achieve human-guided automated control from a single motion prompt.
The key idea is to leverage the Bayesian structure of GPs together with a geometry-aware formulation, ensuring that the learned motion model exhibits invariance properties critical for generalization.
In contrast to deep learning methods, our approach requires only a single demonstration and is robust to variations in the human prompt, accounting for small deviations from the original trajectory.
This one-shot capability directly addresses the data scarcity challenge in LfD and facilitates more intuitive and scalable human–robot interaction.
The contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A novel human-guided automated control framework based on GP models that learn from human demonstrations is proposed, enabling robots to autonomously identify and execute appropriate skills to complete the task with a motion prompt.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The one-shot GP algorithm with coordinate transformation functions is developed that guarantees geometry-invariant multi-step predictions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A comprehensive evaluation, including simulations with a graphical user interface and two real-world robotic experiments, was conducted to demonstrate the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed approach.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dynamic Model</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The joint dynamics of a robot manipulator with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> degrees of freedom is modeled by</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q})\,\ddot{\boldsymbol{q}}+\boldsymbol{C}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})\,\dot{\boldsymbol{q}}+\boldsymbol{D}\dot{\boldsymbol{q}}+\boldsymbol{g}(\boldsymbol{q})+\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})=\boldsymbol{\tau}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝑫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝒅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝝉</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q})\,\ddot{\boldsymbol{q}}+\boldsymbol{C}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})\,\dot{\boldsymbol{q}}+\boldsymbol{D}\dot{\boldsymbol{q}}+\boldsymbol{g}(\boldsymbol{q})+\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})=\boldsymbol{\tau},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the configuration is defined by the joint space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{q}\in\mathcal{C}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{q}\in\mathcal{C}\subset\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑴</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑫</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the inertia, Coriolis/centrifugal, and damping matrices, respectively. The vector <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\boldsymbol{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\boldsymbol{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the gravitational torques acting on the joints.
The control torque vector is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the vector function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the external disturbances and model uncertainties.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task-Space Control</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The manipulator is controlled in the task-space using a Cartesian impedance controller to realize a desired translational twist with a specific scale. The corresponding joint torque command is computed as</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}=\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}\boldsymbol{F}+\boldsymbol{N}(\boldsymbol{q})\boldsymbol{\tau}_{0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑭</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑵</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}=\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}\boldsymbol{F}+\boldsymbol{N}(\boldsymbol{q})\boldsymbol{\tau}_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian matrix, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{F}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑭</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{F}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the command control in the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional task space, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the desired null-space torque.
The first term of the right-hand side in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ II-B Task-Space Control ‣ II Preliminaries ‣ Prompt2Auto: From Motion Prompt to Automated Control via Geometry-Invariant One-Shot Gaussian Process Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) projects the task-space force into joint torque, ensuring the primary task is executed, and the second term enables redundancy resolution by projecting <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\tau}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\tau}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> into the null-space of the Jacobian, thereby ensuring that secondary objectives can be achieved without disturbing the primary task.
The null-space projection matrix is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{N}(\boldsymbol{q})=\boldsymbol{I}-\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑵</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{N}(\boldsymbol{q})=\boldsymbol{I}-\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#})^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity matrix, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the dynamically consistent generalized inverse of the Jacobian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, which is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#}=\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q})^{-1}\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}\left(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q})^{-1}\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}\right)^{-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#}=\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q})^{-1}\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}\left(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})\boldsymbol{M}(\boldsymbol{q})^{-1}\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}\right)^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">To achieve the desired task-space motion tracking, the impedance controller is designed as</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{F}=\boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\boldsymbol{q})\boldsymbol{\ddot{x}}_{d}+\boldsymbol{\Gamma}(\boldsymbol{q},\boldsymbol{\dot{q}})\boldsymbol{\dot{x}}_{d}-\boldsymbol{K}_{d}\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}-\boldsymbol{D}_{d}\dot{\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}}+\boldsymbol{F}_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑭</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{F}=\boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\boldsymbol{q})\boldsymbol{\ddot{x}}_{d}+\boldsymbol{\Gamma}(\boldsymbol{q},\boldsymbol{\dot{q}})\boldsymbol{\dot{x}}_{d}-\boldsymbol{K}_{d}\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}-\boldsymbol{D}_{d}\dot{\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}}+\boldsymbol{F}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">with the task-space inertia matrix, Coriolis/centrifugal matrix, and gravity vector as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\boldsymbol{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\boldsymbol{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(\boldsymbol{J(\boldsymbol{q})M(\boldsymbol{q})}^{-1}\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}\right)^{-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(\boldsymbol{J(\boldsymbol{q})M(\boldsymbol{q})}^{-1}\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\top}\right)^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Gamma}(\boldsymbol{q},\boldsymbol{\dot{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Gamma}(\boldsymbol{q},\boldsymbol{\dot{q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#})^{\top}}\boldsymbol{C}(\boldsymbol{q},\boldsymbol{\dot{q}})\boldsymbol{\dot{q}}-\boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\boldsymbol{q})\dot{\boldsymbol{J}}(\boldsymbol{q})\dot{\boldsymbol{q}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#})^{\top}}\boldsymbol{C}(\boldsymbol{q},\boldsymbol{\dot{q}})\boldsymbol{\dot{q}}-\boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\boldsymbol{q})\dot{\boldsymbol{J}}(\boldsymbol{q})\dot{\boldsymbol{q}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{F}_{g}(\boldsymbol{q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{F}_{g}(\boldsymbol{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle={(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#})^{\top}}\boldsymbol{g}(\boldsymbol{q})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>#</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒈</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle={(\boldsymbol{J}(\boldsymbol{q})^{\#})^{\top}}\boldsymbol{g}(\boldsymbol{q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">respectively, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{K}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{K}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{D}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{D}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive definite stiffness and damping matrices.
The task-space error <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of both position and orientation terms, with
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}=[\boldsymbol{p}^{\top},\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}=[\boldsymbol{p}^{\top},\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding desired values
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{d}=[\boldsymbol{p}_{d}^{\top},\boldsymbol{\phi}_{d}^{\top}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{d}=[\boldsymbol{p}_{d}^{\top},\boldsymbol{\phi}_{d}^{\top}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The position error is given by
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{p}}=\boldsymbol{p}-\boldsymbol{p}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{p}}=\boldsymbol{p}-\boldsymbol{p}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the current position and
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{p}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{p}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by time integration of the commanded translational velocity
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\nu}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝂</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\nu}_{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to its initial task coordinate.
The orientation error is encoded as</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{\phi}}=\tfrac{1}{2}\left(\boldsymbol{R}_{d}^{T}\boldsymbol{R}-\boldsymbol{R}^{T}\boldsymbol{R}_{d}\right)^{\vee}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑹</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{\phi}}=\tfrac{1}{2}\left(\boldsymbol{R}_{d}^{T}\boldsymbol{R}-\boldsymbol{R}^{T}\boldsymbol{R}_{d}\right)^{\vee},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.7" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{R},\boldsymbol{R}_{d}\in SO(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑹</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{R},\boldsymbol{R}_{d}\in SO(3)</annotation></semantics></math> are the current and desired rotation matrices.
The operator <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)^{\vee}:\mathcal{SO}(3)\to\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)^{\vee}:\mathcal{SO}(3)\to\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the vector from the skew-symmetric matrix representation in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{SO}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{SO}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, the closed loop dynamics under the control law with external disturbance <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\boldsymbol{q})\ddot{\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}}+(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}(\boldsymbol{q},\boldsymbol{\dot{q}})+\boldsymbol{D}_{d})\dot{\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}}+\boldsymbol{K}_{d}\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}=\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>¨</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Lambda}(\boldsymbol{q})\ddot{\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}}+(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}(\boldsymbol{q},\boldsymbol{\dot{q}})+\boldsymbol{D}_{d})\dot{\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}}+\boldsymbol{K}_{d}\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}=\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.8" class="ltx_p">where the error state <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes if <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{q},\dot{\boldsymbol{q}})</annotation></semantics></math> gets properly compensated.
Furthermore, the remaining null-space behavior is designed to align with the initial posture <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{q}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{q}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in the null-space through</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\tau}_{0}=-\boldsymbol{K}_{N}(\boldsymbol{q}-\boldsymbol{q}_{0})-\boldsymbol{D}_{N}\dot{\boldsymbol{q}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\tau}_{0}=-\boldsymbol{K}_{N}(\boldsymbol{q}-\boldsymbol{q}_{0})-\boldsymbol{D}_{N}\dot{\boldsymbol{q}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.9" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{K}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{K}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{D}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{D}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are proper null-space stiffness and damping matrices.
Note that the projection <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{N}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ II-B Task-Space Control ‣ II Preliminaries ‣ Prompt2Auto: From Motion Prompt to Automated Control via Geometry-Invariant One-Shot Gaussian Process Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) ensures that this torque does not affect the task-space motion, as it lies in the null-space of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{J}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gaussian Process Regression</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Considering a general vector-valued nonlinear function <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}(\mathbf{x}):\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}(\mathbf{x}):\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, we utilize Gaussian process regression to estimate this unknown function, where the input domain is <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the output domain is <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Without loss of generality, we assume the function <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is composed of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> independent scalar functions, i.e., <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}(\cdot)=[f_{1}(\cdot),\dots,f_{d_{\mathrm{y}}}(\cdot)]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}(\cdot)=[f_{1}(\cdot),\dots,f_{d_{\mathrm{y}}}(\cdot)]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each component <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is modeled as an independent sample from a GP.
This assumes that each function <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS) induced by a positive definite kernel <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}(\cdot,\cdot):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}(\cdot,\cdot):\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To learn the function from data, we first make the following standard assumption about the dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The dataset <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\big\{\big(\mathbf{x}^{(\iota)},\mathbf{y}^{(\iota)}\big)\big\}_{\iota=1,\cdots,N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\big\{\big(\mathbf{x}^{(\iota)},\mathbf{y}^{(\iota)}\big)\big\}_{\iota=1,\cdots,N}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> input-output pairs. The configuration of the robot <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{(\iota)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{(\iota)}</annotation></semantics></math> is measurable, and the outputs <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{(\iota)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{(\iota)}</annotation></semantics></math> are observations of the unknown function <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}</annotation></semantics></math> corrupted by the noise</span></p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{(\iota)}=\boldsymbol{f}(\mathbf{x}^{(\iota)})+\boldsymbol{\omega}^{(\iota)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝝎</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{(\iota)}=\boldsymbol{f}(\mathbf{x}^{(\iota)})+\boldsymbol{\omega}^{(\iota)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}^{(\iota)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝛚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">y</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}^{(\iota)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathrm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the measurement noise.
For each dimension <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\dots,d_{\mathrm{y}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\dots,d_{\mathrm{y}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the noise component <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}^{(\iota)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}^{(\iota)}</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to be bounded, i.e., <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\omega_{i}^{(\iota)}|\leq\bar{\sigma}_{n,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\omega_{i}^{(\iota)}|\leq\bar{\sigma}_{n,i}</annotation></semantics></math> for a known constant <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\sigma}_{n,i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\sigma}_{n,i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each dimension <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the dataset as <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{i}=\{(\mathbf{x}^{(\iota)},\mathbf{y}_{i}^{(\iota)})\}_{\iota=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{i}=\{(\mathbf{x}^{(\iota)},\mathbf{y}_{i}^{(\iota)})\}_{\iota=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{i}=[\mathrm{y}_{i}^{(1)},\dots,\mathrm{y}_{i}^{(N)}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{i}=[\mathrm{y}_{i}^{(1)},\dots,\mathrm{y}_{i}^{(N)}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector of training outputs for the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th dimension.
Assume the posterior distribution for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> at a new test point <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{*}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{*}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is Gaussian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, then its mean <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*}|\mathcal{D}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*}|\mathcal{D}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}^{2}(\mathbf{x}^{*}|\mathcal{D}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}^{2}(\mathbf{x}^{*}|\mathcal{D}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are calculated as follows</p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E13.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*}|\mathcal{D}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*}|\mathcal{D}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{k}_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{K}_{i}+\sigma_{n,i}^{2}\boldsymbol{I})^{-1}\mathbf{y}_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{k}_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{K}_{i}+\sigma_{n,i}^{2}\boldsymbol{I})^{-1}\mathbf{y}_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E13.x1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.x1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{i}^{2}(\mathbf{x}^{*}|\mathcal{D}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{i}^{2}(\mathbf{x}^{*}|\mathcal{D}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.x1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\kappa_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*},\mathbf{x}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\kappa_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*},\mathbf{x}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E13.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E13.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad-\boldsymbol{k}_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{K}_{i}+\sigma_{n,i}^{2}\boldsymbol{I})^{-1}\boldsymbol{k}_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad-\boldsymbol{k}_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{K}_{i}+\sigma_{n,i}^{2}\boldsymbol{I})^{-1}\boldsymbol{k}_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{k}_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})=[\kappa_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*},\mathbf{x}^{(1)}),\dots,\kappa_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*},\mathbf{x}^{(N)})]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{k}_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})=[\kappa_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*},\mathbf{x}^{(1)}),\dots,\kappa_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*},\mathbf{x}^{(N)})]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector of kernel evaluations between the test point and training points, and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{K}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{K}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> Gram matrix with entries <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\boldsymbol{K}_{i}]_{js}=\kappa_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{(j)},\mathbf{x}^{(s)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\boldsymbol{K}_{i}]_{js}=\kappa_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{(j)},\mathbf{x}^{(s)})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Method</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Geometric Invariance Prediction with One-Shot GPs</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on the task of multi-step trajectory prediction in a planar workspace using a one-shot GP approach, primarily for clarity of exposition.
In this setting, the GP model is trained once on a historical dataset obtained from a single demonstration provided by the user.
The primary challenge in this one-shot setting is that standard GP methods typically require multiple demonstrations to generalize effectively and are sensitive to the absolute positions of the trajectories.
This makes them unable to handle variations in the starting point or unseen but similar motion patterns.
To overcome this limitation, the core idea is to move away from predicting absolute positions in a global coordinate frame.
Instead, we represent the trajectory in polar coordinates and use GP regression to learn a mapping from historical increments (differences) to the future increments of the trajectory.
By focusing on relative changes rather than absolute positions with coordinate transformation, the learned model becomes invariant to translation, rotation, and scaling of the trajectory, enabling robust prediction even when the motion prompt deviates from the original demonstration.
This geometric-invariant formulation allows the system to generalize from a single human demonstration while supporting accurate multi-step prediction.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>1 </span>Coordinate Transformation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to establish the training dataset, we measure the joint configuration as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{q}_{\omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{q}_{\omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{q}_{\omega}=\boldsymbol{q}+\boldsymbol{\omega}_{q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{q}_{\omega}=\boldsymbol{q}+\boldsymbol{\omega}_{q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the measurement noise, bounded by a known constant <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\omega}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\omega}_{q}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\boldsymbol{\omega}_{q}\|\leq\bar{\omega}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\boldsymbol{\omega}_{q}\|\leq\bar{\omega}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding end-effector position in the task space is obtained through the forward kinematics and projection as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{p}=\boldsymbol{\Pi}\,\mathbf{T}(\boldsymbol{q}_{\omega})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{p}=\boldsymbol{\Pi}\,\mathbf{T}(\boldsymbol{q}_{\omega}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}(\cdot):\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}(\cdot):\mathbb{R}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the forward kinematics mapping from the joint configuration to the 3D Cartesian position of the end-effector. The matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Pi}\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Pi}\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection operator that maps 3D Cartesian coordinates onto the 2D task space coordinates.
In the case of projection onto the planar surface X-Y, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Pi}=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;0\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\Pi}=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;0&amp;0\\
0&amp;1&amp;0\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">To eliminate the translational and rotational dependency, we establish a polar coordinate system with the origin fixed at the initial observation point <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}(t_{0})=[\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{0}),\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{0})]^{\top}=\mathbf{0}_{2\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}(t_{0})=[\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{0}),\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{0})]^{\top}=\mathbf{0}_{2\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Cartesian coordinates, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
All subsequent spatial coordinates are then expressed relative to this reference frame.
Given the discrete-time data acquisition process, the temporal index <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is employed with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote successive observations.
Within this polar coordinate, we define the radial distance and angular orientation as follows</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{(\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{k})-\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{0}))^{2}+(\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{k})-\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{0}))^{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{(\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{k})-\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{0}))^{2}+(\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{k})-\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{0}))^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\theta(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{atan2}(\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{k})-\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{0}),\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{k})-\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{0}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>atan2</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{atan2}(\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{k})-\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{0}),\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{k})-\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{0})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the Euclidean distance from the initial position to the current observation at discrete time <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the angular displacement measured counterclockwise from the positive <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{x}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{x}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-axis using the arctangent function.
The origin polar dataset is then denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{polar}}=\{(r(t_{k}),\theta(t_{k}))\}_{k\in\mathbb{N}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>polar</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{polar}}=\{(r(t_{k}),\theta(t_{k}))\}_{k\in\mathbb{N}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The final three-dimensional feature vector is constructed through continuous angle embedding, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\xi}(t_{k})=[r(t_{k}),\cos\theta(t_{k}),\sin\theta(t_{k})]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\xi}(t_{k})=[r(t_{k}),\cos\theta(t_{k}),\sin\theta(t_{k})]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This representation addresses the inherent periodicity of angular measurements by decomposing the angle into its sine and cosine components, thereby avoiding discontinuities at the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math> boundary while preserving the complete angular information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance the generalizability of our approach, we implement data normalization by scaling all feature values to the range.
For the radial component, which is inherently non-negative, we employ</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{r}(t_{k})={r(t_{k})}/{r}_{\text{max}}\in\tilde{\mathcal{R}}\subset\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{r}(t_{k})={r(t_{k})}/{r}_{\text{max}}\in\tilde{\mathcal{R}}\subset\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{r}_{\text{max}}=\max_{t}r(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{r}_{\text{max}}=\max_{t}r(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the trigonometric components, which naturally lie in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply an affine transformation</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{\cos\theta}(t_{k})=\frac{\cos\theta(t_{k})+1}{2},\widetilde{\sin\theta}(t_{k})=\frac{\sin\theta(t_{k})+1}{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{\cos\theta}(t_{k})=\frac{\cos\theta(t_{k})+1}{2},\widetilde{\sin\theta}(t_{k})=\frac{\sin\theta(t_{k})+1}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The normalized feature vector becomes</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})=[\tilde{r}(t_{k}),\widetilde{\cos\theta}(t_{k}),\widetilde{\sin\theta}(t_{k})]^{\top}\in\tilde{\mathcal{X}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})=[\tilde{r}(t_{k}),\widetilde{\cos\theta}(t_{k}),\widetilde{\sin\theta}(t_{k})]^{\top}\in\tilde{\mathcal{X}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">which ensures that all the normalized features are <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\xi}_{i}(t_{k})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ξ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\xi}_{i}(t_{k})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>2 </span>Dataset Construction</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of utilizing absolute coordinate sequences directly, an augmented input vector is constructed that incorporates both absolute positions and related velocities. This enriches the representation of the input states for providing the Gaussian Process with more informative descriptions.
Specifically, for a given time step <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct the feature vector by augmenting the normalized absolute polar features with incremental components as follows</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\zeta}(t_{k})=[\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})^{\top},~\Delta\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})^{\top}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{6}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\zeta}(t_{k})=[\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})^{\top},~\Delta\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})^{\top}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{6},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k}):=[\Delta\tilde{r}(t_{k}),\Delta\widetilde{\cos\theta}(t_{k}),\Delta\widetilde{\sin\theta}(t_{k})]^{\top}=(\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})-\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k-1}))/(t_{k}-t_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k}):=[\Delta\tilde{r}(t_{k}),\Delta\widetilde{\cos\theta}(t_{k}),\Delta\widetilde{\sin\theta}(t_{k})]^{\top}=(\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})-\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k-1}))/(t_{k}-t_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the discrete-time velocity in normalized feature space and the initial value is defined as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\zeta}(t_{0})=\boldsymbol{0}_{6\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\zeta}(t_{0})=\boldsymbol{0}_{6\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math>
Notably, while acceleration or higher-order derivatives could in principle be included, for practical stability and robustness, using only the position and its discrete-time increments is sufficient to achieve accurate predictions in real-world applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To capture temporal dependencies in the trajectory data, we employ a sliding window approach with window length <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The input vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\zeta}(t_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{3w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\zeta}(t_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{3w}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed by concatenating the most recent <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> feature vectors as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k},w)=[{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k-w})^{\top},{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k-w+1})^{\top},\ldots,{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k-1})^{\top}]^{\top},\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜻</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k},w)=[{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k-w})^{\top},{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k-w+1})^{\top},\ldots,{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k-1})^{\top}]^{\top},\!</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq w</annotation></semantics></math>.
This concatenation results in a <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3w</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional input vector that encodes both the spatial characteristics and the temporal evolution of the trajectory over the preceding <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> time steps.
Therefore, the augmented dataset is constructed as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{aug}}(t_{k},w)=\{(\tilde{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{i},w),\Delta\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{i})^{\top})\}_{i\in[0,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>aug</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{aug}}(t_{k},w)=\{(\tilde{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{i},w),\Delta\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{i})^{\top})\}_{i\in[0,k)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address the multi-output prediction task, we employ three independent GP models for each dimension of the target vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This architecture decomposes the prediction problem into three separate regression tasks, each conditioned on the same input state vector <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k},w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k},w)</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{GP}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{GP}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\hat{r}(t_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\hat{r}(t_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{GP}_{\cos}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>cos</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{GP}_{\cos}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\widehat{\cos\theta}(t_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\widehat{\cos\theta}(t_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{GP}_{\sin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>sin</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{GP}_{\sin}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\widehat{\sin\theta}(t_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\widehat{\sin\theta}(t_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then we define <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot)=[{\mu}_{\text{r}}(\cdot),{\mu}_{\cos}(\cdot),{\mu}_{\sin}(\cdot)]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>r</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>cos</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>sin</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mu}(\cdot)=[{\mu}_{\text{r}}(\cdot),{\mu}_{\cos}(\cdot),{\mu}_{\sin}(\cdot)]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> for reconstructing the one-step absolute prediction of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\boldsymbol{\xi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\boldsymbol{\xi}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are calculated as follows</p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k+1})=\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})+\boldsymbol{\mu}(\tilde{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k},w)|\mathcal{D}_{\text{aug}}(t_{f},w))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>aug</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k+1})=\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})+\boldsymbol{\mu}(\tilde{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}(t_{k},w)|\mathcal{D}_{\text{aug}}(t_{f},w)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝃</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{\xi}}(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the measurement at the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th time step, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the time of the last measurement included in the augmented dataset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{aug}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>aug</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{aug}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
After performing the coordinate transformation and constructing the dataset, predictions can be generated within the normalized domain <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this approach inherently assumes that the prompt motion is on the same scale as the demonstration, which is generally infeasible because the prompt motion cannot perfectly replicate the demonstrated trajectory.
Moreover, the user may terminate the input at any arbitrary point, meaning that the prompt motion does not necessarily cover the entire trajectory observed in the demonstration.
Therefore, a scaling strategy is required to adapt the prediction process to varying input lengths and scales.
To determine the scale factor, we propose a deterministic approach based on anchor points.
Considering the angular invariance during the scaling, we define an angular sequence with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> predefined checkpoints by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\check{\Theta}=\{\check{\theta}_{i}\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{polar}}|i=1,\dots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>polar</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\Theta}=\{\check{\theta}_{i}\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{polar}}|i=1,\dots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math> and its corresponding radial sequence by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\check{\mathcal{R}}=\{\check{r}_{i}\in\tilde{\mathcal{R}}|i=1,\dots,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathcal{R}}=\{\check{r}_{i}\in\tilde{\mathcal{R}}|i=1,\dots,C\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At time step <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the index of the last reached checkpoint as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C(t_{k})=\max\{i\in\{1,\dots,C\}\mid t_{\check{\theta}_{i}}\leq t_{k}\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C(t_{k})=\max\{i\in\{1,\dots,C\}\mid t_{\check{\theta}_{i}}\leq t_{k}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\check{\theta}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\check{\theta}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the time at which checkpoint <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\theta}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\theta}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs.
The scale factor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is then computed as the average ratio of the observed to the reference radial distances up to this checkpoint, as follows</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda(t_{k})=\frac{1}{C(t_{k})}\sum_{i=1}^{C(t_{k})}\frac{r(t_{i})}{\check{r}_{i}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda(t_{k})=\frac{1}{C(t_{k})}\sum_{i=1}^{C(t_{k})}\frac{r(t_{i})}{\check{r}_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Building upon this scale factor and considering the normalization functions (<a href="#S3.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ III-A1 Coordinate Transformation ‣ III-A Geometric Invariance Prediction with One-Shot GPs ‣ III Method ‣ Prompt2Auto: From Motion Prompt to Automated Control via Geometry-Invariant One-Shot Gaussian Process Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ III-A1 Coordinate Transformation ‣ III-A Geometric Invariance Prediction with One-Shot GPs ‣ III Method ‣ Prompt2Auto: From Motion Prompt to Automated Control via Geometry-Invariant One-Shot Gaussian Process Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>), we are able to make consistent predictions within the transformed domain <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are calculated as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{r}(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{r}(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda(t_{k})\hat{r}(t_{k})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda(t_{k})\hat{r}(t_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{\theta}(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{\theta}(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{atan2}(2{\widehat{\sin\theta}}(t_{k})-1,2{\widehat{\cos\theta}}(t_{k})-1)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>atan2</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{atan2}(2{\widehat{\sin\theta}}(t_{k})-1,2{\widehat{\cos\theta}}(t_{k})-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.5" class="ltx_p">Consequently, the predictions of the planar position <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{p}}(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{p}}(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> at time <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in Cartesian coordinates are given as,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{p}}(t_{k+1})=\hat{\mathbf{p}}(t_{k})+\bar{r}(t_{k})[\cos{\bar{\theta}},\sin{\bar{\theta}}]^{\top}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{p}}(t_{k+1})=\hat{\mathbf{p}}(t_{k})+\bar{r}(t_{k})[\cos{\bar{\theta}},\sin{\bar{\theta}}]^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> One Step Prediction with GeoGP</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              Window length <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, data quantity <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{aug}}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>aug</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{aug}}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase 1: Data Preprocessing</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              Set origin at initial position: <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}(t_{0})=[\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{0}),\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{0})]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}(t_{0})=[\mathrm{p}_{1}(t_{0}),\mathrm{p}_{2}(t_{0})]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(t_{k}),\theta(t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(t_{k}),\theta(t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <a href="#S3.E17" title="In III-A1 Coordinate Transformation ‣ III-A Geometric Invariance Prediction with One-Shot GPs ‣ III Method ‣ Prompt2Auto: From Motion Prompt to Automated Control via Geometry-Invariant One-Shot Gaussian Process Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eqs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> and <a href="#S3.E18" title="Equation 18 ‣ III-A1 Coordinate Transformation ‣ III-A Geometric Invariance Prediction with One-Shot GPs ‣ III Method ‣ Prompt2Auto: From Motion Prompt to Automated Control via Geometry-Invariant One-Shot Gaussian Process Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>

